% A declared `any` nested inside a type must merge across a shared
% variable exactly as a top-level one does, in either head order.
%
% X's two declaration sources are list(any) and list(int). Per §Type
% states the variable keeps its most informative source, so X is
% list(int) and passing it to s/1 is an inconsistency — whichever head
% position comes first.
%
% The two rules differ only in the order of the head conjunction, so
% they must draw the same diagnostic. `any_first` used to type-check
% clean: the slot was bound whole to list(any) by the first head, and
% the merge then compared an `any` that had nowhere left to go.

:- module(nested_any_merge_order, []).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

:- chr_constraint p(list(any)), q(list(int)), s(list(string)).

any_first @ p(X), q(X) <=> s(X).
int_first @ q(Y), p(Y) <=> s(Y).
